  },
];
---

<PageShell
  page={page}
  h1="Dubai mortgage brokers: what they cost and when to use one"
  answer={answer}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>Who pays the broker, and what does that change?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Going through a broker compared with going direct"
    columns={['', 'Through a broker', 'Direct to the bank']}
    rows={[
      ['Who pays', 'The lender, 0.5–1% of the loan', 'Nobody — you deal with the bank'],
      ['Cost to you', 'Usually nothing directly', 'Nothing'],
      ['Lenders considered', 'Several, filtered against your profile', 'One'],
      ['Best when', 'Your income or status is non-standard', 'You already know you qualify'],
      ['Main risk', 'The incentive is completion, not the lowest total cost', 'You never see a better offer'],
      ['Your time', 'Lower', 'Higher'],
    ]}
    source="Standard Dubai mortgage intermediation fee structure"
  />

  <p>
    The 0.5–1% is the number to hold on to, because it explains the behaviour you will
    encounter. A broker is paid when a loan completes. That aligns with you most of the time —
    you also want the loan to complete — and diverges precisely when the right answer is
    "borrow less" or "wait".
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not an argument against using one. It is an argument for asking, at the point of
    recommendation, which lenders were considered and why the recommended one won.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the current rates and criteria?</h2>

  <p>
    Rates, loan-to-value caps and lender criteria move often enough that publishing them
    without a date attached is worse than not publishing them. Current figures from named
    lenders belong here:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="current UAE mortgage rates and LTV caps by lender, with the date each was published" />
  </p>

  <p>
    What does not move is the structure of the decision, which is what the rest of this page
    covers.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is a broker worth it for?</h2>

  <p><strong>Worth it if:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>You are self-employed, or your income is structured unusually</li>
    <li>You are paid outside the UAE, or in more than one currency</li>
    <li>You are buying off-plan, or a property type banks treat cautiously</li>
    <li>You have been declined once already and do not know why</li>
    <li>You do not have the time to run several applications yourself</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Probably not worth it if:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>You are salaried with a UAE employer and a clean credit position</li>
    <li>You already bank with a lender offering competitive terms to existing customers</li>
    <li>You are buying a standard completed apartment in an established community</li>
    <li>You have the time and inclination to compare offers yourself</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Disqualifying yourself honestly is more useful than a page that recommends a broker to
    everyone. Roughly half the people reading this do not need one.
  </p>

  <h2>What should you ask before appointing one?</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Who pays you, and how much, on a loan of this size?</li>
    <li>Do you charge me a fee as well as receiving one from the lender?</li>
    <li>Which lenders did you consider, and why did the recommended one win?</li>
    <li>Which authority are you registered with, and what is the registration number?</li>
    <li>What happens if the valuation comes in below the purchase price?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The last one matters more than it sounds. A lender lends against a valuation, not against
    the price agreed, and a shortfall becomes cash you have to find at short notice. If you are
    buying, knowing the property’s evidenced value before that stage is not an academic
    exercise.
  </p>

  <h2>What about borrowing from overseas?</h2>

  <p>
    Non-residents face lower loan-to-value caps, a shorter list of lenders and heavier
    documentation requirements. That is a different process rather than a variation on this
    one, and it is covered on{' '}
    <a href="/blog/non-resident-mortgage-dubai/">non-resident mortgages in Dubai</a>.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If you already own and are weighing whether to refinance or sell, "
    anchor="a written valuation of the property"
    after=" is the number that comparison has to start from."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
